{
  "image_name": "2285e61081dc3805166f547b4527509d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes applications of chiral and polarization-sensitive photonic materials. Overall, it shows how controlling the handedness and polarization state of light can be useful across spectroscopy, security, imaging, sensing, displays, holography, quantum optics, and light detection.\n\nSeveral top schematics highlight broad uses: enhanced photophysical studies through chiral spectra, anti-counterfeiting materials that reveal security features under illumination, higher-contrast microscopy using a chiral dye around a cell, and optical sensing based on selective interaction with one polarization state. The central and largest part explains a multilayer optical stack built from a linear polarizer, half-wave plate, quarter-wave plate, and metallic mirror. The upper pathway illustrates an anti-glare filter: incoming ambient light is transformed between linear and circular polarization states so that reflected light is attenuated rather than returned to the viewer. The lower pathway compares a normal OLED with a circularly polarized OLED (CP-OLED), showing that a conventional design loses about half the emitted light at the polarizer, whereas the CP-OLED configuration can in principle reach full efficiency by matching the device emission to the polarization optics.\n\nThe bottom icons indicate further uses in holography, quantum technologies, and detectors that distinguish circularly polarized light.",
